{:A {:score 7
     :reason "Hal mostly sounds like the GDD's Hal. 'Welcome back.', 'Last call. You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}.' and 'You said {habit}: {goal}. Want today to count toward it?' are warm and short, they speak to you, and there are no film quotes or job titles. Points come off where Hal explains the system or reads out data instead of talking ('The crew works with the game closed too', 'Your words: {habit}, {goal}.'), for one butler-like 'noted', and for three lines that claim things the event doesn't carry."
     :violations [{:where ":jack-in [2] ‘Here you are. The crew works with the game closed too; let's see where things stand.’"
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "Explains a design rule like a tutorial instead of saying what happened. A companion doesn't lecture about how the game works."
                   :fix "Here you are. Let's see what the crew got done while you were out."}
                  {:where ":coach-habit [2] ‘Your words: {habit}, {goal}. No pressure, just checking in.’"
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "'Your words: {habit}, {goal}.' reads like a form being read back ('Your words: workout, 3/wk'), not a companion using your goal the way the GDD's 'You said three workouts a week. One today?' does."
                   :fix "You told me {habit}, {goal}. No pressure; one today?"}
                  {:where ":stay-late [3] ‘Staying late, noted. No more asking tonight.’"
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "'Noted' is how a butler or clerk talks; the rule asks for a companion."
                   :fix "Staying late, then. I won't ask again tonight."}
                  {:where ":weekly-card [0] ‘{hours} in this week. Fewer hours, same life getting done: that's the curve we want.’"
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "'Fewer hours' claims the trend is going down, but the event carries no trend value. In a week where hours went up, this line is false."
                   :fix "Time jacked in this week: {hours}. The aim's fewer hours with life still getting done."}
                  {:where ":agent-stalled [1] ‘{agent} stopped reporting, so I marked it stalled. No busy glow pretending.’"
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "Per §3.1 the bridge marks an agent stalled on its own, so Hal saying 'I marked it' misstates what really happened. 'No busy glow pretending' is also garbled."
                   :fix "{agent} stopped reporting, so it shows stalled now, not busy."}
                  {:where ":chore-undone [3] ‘Easy mistake. {name}'s {chore}'s back on the board.’"
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "A chore lives on the creature's mirror, not the notice board, so 'back on the board' makes up a place. The double possessive ('Flora's feed's') is also clunky."
                   :fix "Easy fix. {name}'s {chore} is open again, like the tick never happened."}]}
 :B {:score 9
     :reason "The world rules hold up well. Closing time is your own rule ('It's {bedtime}, the hour you picked.'), jacking out is good ('{name} comes first. This waits.', 'the estate keeps'), blueprints wait unchanged ('Nothing changes till you approve it'), SAMPLE/REAL shows on the badge, and stalls are stated plainly without apology ('Any keycards it held came back to you.'). One stall line blurs what the limit means."
     :violations [{:where ":agent-stalled [0] ‘{agent} hit the cap and went quiet. Desk's freed till it refills.’"
                   :rule 10
                   :issue "'Freed till it refills' links the freed desk to the cap refilling. In §3.1 and §2.3 the desk is freed for other work right away and the cap refills separately, so the limit isn't stated clearly."
                   :fix "{agent} ran out of juice and went quiet. Its desk is freed; the cap refills later."}]}
 :C {:score 8
     :reason "There are no XP, streaks, purchases, sent messages or private details anywhere. The memorial lines are tender and never give {name} needs ('Nothing more will ask you about {name}.'), and :chore-undone stays free of blame ('No harm done.'). The weekly card slips twice: 'Down is the good way' passes judgement on a week where hours went up, and 'Just a trend, not a score' puts scoring language in the bubble."
     :violations [{:where ":weekly-card [3] ‘On the notice board: {hours} jacked in, and which way it's heading. Down is the good way.’"
                   :rule 12
                   :issue "'Down is the good way' grades the week, so it reads as a quiet scolding whenever hours went up. The weekly card should show the trend, not judge it."
                   :fix "On the notice board: {hours} jacked in, and which way it's heading."}
                  {:where ":weekly-card [1] ‘Time in this week: {hours}. Just a trend, not a score.’"
                   :rule 11
                   :issue "Saying 'not a score' still brings the idea of a score into the bubble. §1 says the card is shown 'never as a score', which means not framing it that way at all."
                   :fix "Time in this week: {hours}. The board shows how it's trending."}]}
 :D {:score 5
     :reason "Most lines fit a bubble and feel cozy and specific ('The day fits on one card; I counted {count} on it.'). But two closing lines run to three sentences, several placeholders only read right with some values ({text} dropped in raw, a bare {hours}, 'the {chore}' in some lines and '{chore}'s' in another), and six keys repeat one line reworded instead of saying different things."
     :violations [{:where ":closing-time [0] ‘{bedtime}, like you asked. Estate's going to night. Goodnight.’"
                   :rule 16
                   :issue "Three sentences, over the one-or-two limit for a bubble."
                   :fix "{bedtime}, like you asked. The estate's going to night; goodnight."}
                  {:where ":closing-time [2] ‘{bedtime}'s here. Night's falling. Goodnight.’"
                   :rule 16
                   :issue "Three sentences, and it rewords [0] ('going to night' / 'Night's falling'), which also breaks rule 18."
                   :fix "{bedtime}. The crew keeps working; goodnight, see you at breakfast."}
                  {:where ":jack-out-household (all four lines)"
                   :rule 19
                   :issue "{text} is dropped in raw, so each line reads right only if {text} brings its own end punctuation ('Someone at home: Dad ride Off you go'). 'Hear that? {text} Nothing here needs you more.' is also three sentences."
                   :fix "Put {text} in quotes and follow it with a dash, e.g. ‘Phone booth: “{text}” — go on, this keeps.’ and ‘Hear that? “{text}” — nothing here needs you more.’"}
                  {:where ":coming-back [0]–[3]"
                   :rule 19
                   :issue "The article changes between lines: [0]–[2] say 'the {chore}' and [3] says '{chore}'s', so with chore 'feed' you get both 'The feed counted' and 'feed's taken care of'. [3] is also a reword of [0] (rule 18)."
                   :fix "Use one shape throughout, and replace [3] with a line that offers the next small step as in §2.3: ‘You're back. That {chore} counted; want the next small step?’"}
                  {:where ":weekly-card [2] ‘{hours} jacked in this week — part of the curve, nothing more.’"
                   :rule 19
                   :issue "If {hours} is a bare number, this reads '6 jacked in this week'. Only a label-first line reads right whether or not {hours} includes its unit."
                   :fix "Jacked-in time this week: {hours}. Part of the curve, nothing more."}
                  {:where ":blueprint-ready [2] ‘Nothing changes till you approve it. The blueprints are on the shelf.’"
                   :rule 19
                   :issue "The singular 'it' points back to the plural 'blueprints', so the line doesn't agree with itself."
                   :fix "Nothing changes till you approve. The blueprints wait on the shelf."}
                  {:where ":program-loaded [2] ‘That's {program} on now. {building} just came up.’ and [3] ‘{program} loaded. There's {building}.’"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "Both reword [0] ('loaded, building rising') and add nothing new."
                   :fix "[2] ‘{program}'s on my belt now; walk over to {building} when you like.’ [3] ‘Loaded {program}. Next time a task needs it, I won't have to ask.’"}
                  {:where ":program-needed [2] ‘Need {program} for this. Your call.’ and [3] ‘Can't do this one without {program}.’"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "Both restate [1] ('I don't have {program} yet'). [3] also drops the ask that §2.6 calls for."
                   :fix "[2] ‘I need a program for this one: {program}. Want the sample first?’ [3] ‘Load {program} whenever you're ready; this task waits till then.’"}
                  {:where ":stay-late [2] ‘Alright, you're staying up. I only ask once.’"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "Says the same thing as [3] ('No more asking tonight'), so two of the four lines carry one idea."
                   :fix "You're staying. The estate stays at night, and the crew keeps working."}
                  {:where ":chore-undone [1] ‘Undone. {name}'s {chore} goes back on the list.’"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "[0], [1] and [2] all say 'taken back, open again' in different words."
                   :fix "Undone. Nothing's lost; I'll remind you when {name}'s {chore} comes due."}
                  {:where ":chore-not-due [0]–[3]"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "All four lines give {last-at} then {next-at} in the same pattern, so the variants differ only in wording."
                   :fix "Keep [0] and vary the rest, e.g. [3] ‘Too soon to count another. The window opens again around {next-at}.’"}
                  {:where ":jack-in [4] ‘You're jacked in. Let's look around.’"
                   :rule 18
                   :issue "Rewords [1] ('You're in. Let's see…'), and three of the five jack-in lines lean on 'Let's see'."
                   :fix "You're jacked in. Start with the board, or just walk."}]}
 :S {:score 8
     :reason "Most keys clearly belong to Prime Meridian: the phone booth and the hardline, the notice board, the shelf of ghosted blueprints in one tray, keycards coming back, night-shift orders, the SAMPLE/REAL badge, and 'You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}.' A few lines could sit in any cozy game ('Go see to {name}. We'll hold here.', '{text} Go on, we're fine.', 'You're staying. No argument from me.'), even where the estate's own words were easy to use."
